WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:13.068
"Umm . . . " Cody blinked in surprise.

2
00:00:13.069 --> 00:00:17.315
"There's a woman talking to a butterfly in our garden."

3
00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:22.590
Mom laughed. "It's Jan, our new neighbor.

4
00:00:22.591 --> 00:00:28.428
She studies butterflies, and I knew you would be interested in meeting her.

5
00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:32.537
She's tagging some monarchs."

6
00:00:32.538 --> 00:00:38.893
"What?" Cody was fascinated by butterflies, but he had never heard of tagging them.

7
00:00:38.894 --> 00:00:42.966
He hurried outside.

8
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:45.017
Jan smiled at him.

9
00:00:45.018 --> 00:00:49.920
In one hand she held a large orange and black butterfly.

10
00:00:49.921 --> 00:00:56.284
"You must be Cody. Your mom told me you planted this garden."

11
00:00:56.285 --> 00:00:58.530
"Yeah," Cody said.

12
00:00:58.531 --> 00:01:02.701
"Last year my class raised monarch butterflies as a project.

13
00:01:02.702 --> 00:01:07.138
We watched them go from eggs to caterpillars to cocoons to butterflies.

14
00:01:07.139 --> 00:01:12.585
We also learned how they migrate south every fall and north every spring.

15
00:01:12.586 --> 00:01:19.660
So I made a butterfly garden, full of nectar flowers and milkweed, to attract monarchs."

16
00:01:19.661 --> 00:01:23.758
"That's a great way to help butterflies!" Jan said.

17
00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:31.571
"Milkweed is the only plant they can lay their eggs on and the only food newly hatched caterpillars can eat."

18
00:01:31.572 --> 00:01:35.646
She looked down and murmured a few words to the butterfly.

19
00:01:35.647 --> 00:01:44.711
"I always wish them safe travels," she explained, "to help them migrate safely to Mexico."

20
00:01:44.712 --> 00:01:50.099
Cody thought about the butterfly's long journey as he watched it flutter away.

21
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.810
In autumn, monarch butterflies fly south.

22
00:01:54.811 --> 00:02:00.086
It takes them a little more than a month to reach the Mexico border.

23
00:02:00.087 --> 00:02:08.192
When this generation arrives at their winter spots, they rest and save energy.

24
00:02:08.193 --> 00:02:13.666
In spring they lay eggs before they die.

25
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:18.092
The newborn generation then migrates north.

26
00:02:18.093 --> 00:02:26.462
But unlike monarchs in fall, spring butterflies take several generations to make the whole trip north.

27
00:02:26.463 --> 00:02:30.945
Each generation only lives for a few weeks.

28
00:02:30.946 --> 00:02:36.041
Then, the next fall, the cycle repeats.

29
00:02:36.042 --> 00:02:38.789
"My mother said you were tagging butterflies.

30
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.928
What does that mean?" Cody asked.

31
00:02:41.929 --> 00:02:47.504
"Each fall, volunteers tag monarchs to track their migration south."

32
00:02:47.505 --> 00:02:52.240
Jan pulled a sheet of stickers and a clipboard from her bag.

33
00:02:52.241 --> 00:02:55.713
"These stickers are the tags."

34
00:02:55.714 --> 00:03:01.289
They were tiny and round, each one printed with a different code.

35
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:06.826
"I record the code here." She showed him the chart on the clipboard.

36
00:03:06.827 --> 00:03:15.370
Cody saw columns for recording the sticker code, date, location, and other information about the butterfly.

37
00:03:15.371 --> 00:03:20.740
"It's too bad monarchs can't stay here all year long," Cody said.

38
00:03:20.741 --> 00:03:25.799
"They can't survive cold winters, and there's not enough food," Jan said.

39
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:32.909
"Luckily for them, forests in central Mexico offer a perfect environment for them to spend winter.

40
00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:37.760
And they'll be back next spring to enjoy more milkweed." She smiled.

41
00:03:37.761 --> 00:03:43.850
"Maybe someday, you can visit Mexico to see the monarchs' winter spots."

42
00:03:43.851 --> 00:03:45.812
"That would be awesome!"

43
00:03:45.813 --> 00:03:51.316
Cody had seen pictures of trees completely covered with butterflies.

44
00:03:51.317 --> 00:03:55.317
"What do the scientists do with the information from the tags?"

45
00:03:55.318 --> 00:04:00.072
"Scientists visit the monarchs' winter spots and search for tagged butterflies.

46
00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:05.799
Then the information is entered into a computer database," Jan said.

47
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:11.414
"All the data helps to track migration patterns and understand more about monarchs.

48
00:04:11.415 --> 00:04:14.410
The program started in the 1930s.

49
00:04:14.411 --> 00:04:19.326
Now thousands of people in North America volunteer every fall."

50
00:04:19.327 --> 00:04:22.818
"Can I try to tag one?" Cody asked.

51
00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:25.872
Jan handed him her net.

52
00:04:25.873 --> 00:04:30.916
Cody watched another butterfly drop toward a flower.

53
00:04:30.917 --> 00:04:36.252
He swished the net through the air, trapping the insect inside.

54
00:04:36.253 --> 00:04:42.848
He cautiously reached into the net and grasped the butterfly's wings with his fingers.

55
00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:45.911
"This is a male," Cody said.

56
00:04:45.912 --> 00:04:51.301
He had noticed the tiny black spots in the center of the hind wings.

57
00:04:51.302 --> 00:04:54.924
Females didn't have the same markings.

58
00:04:54.925 --> 00:04:58.503
Jan added the information to the chart.

59
00:04:58.504 --> 00:05:05.459
Then she passed a sticker to Cody and pointed to the underside of one of the butterfly's wings.

60
00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:10.268
"The sticker should be placed here where it won't interfere with flying."

61
00:05:10.269 --> 00:05:16.013
Cody pressed the sticker firmly into place. "Finished!"

62
00:05:16.014 --> 00:05:18.635
"Great job!" Jan said.

63
00:05:18.636 --> 00:05:24.204
"Later in the year, I can look online and see if any of the butterflies I tagged were spotted.

64
00:05:24.205 --> 00:05:26.854
I'll let you know if this one is found."

65
00:05:26.855 --> 00:05:31.063
Cody started to open his fingers, then hesitated.

66
00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:36.676
"Good luck!" he whispered before releasing the monarch.

67
00:05:36.677 --> 00:05:43.433
It flew away, ready to begin its long journey south.

